-- [M03-0001] Overview
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Package/type: DBMS_MVIEW
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/100.dbms-mview-overview-mysql.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/100.dbms-mview-overview-mysql.md
--   Overview
--   The DBMS_MVIEW system package allows you to refresh materialized views and purge materialized view logs.
--   Applicability
--   This topic applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition provides only MySQL-compatible mode.
--   Subprograms
--   The following table describes the DBMS_MVIEW subprograms supported by the current OceanBase Database version.
--   Subprogram | Description
--   PURGE_LOG | Purges materialized view logs related to the specified master table (base table).
--   REFRESH | Refreshes the specified materialized view.
--   KILL_REFRESH | Terminates a materialized view refresh batch that is still in progress and managed by the new scheduling framework.
--   REFRESH_REPORT | Generates a hierarchical diagnostic report for a single materialized view refresh task.

-- [M03-0002] PURGE_LOG
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Package/type: DBMS_MVIEW
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/200.purge-log-mysql.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/200.purge-log-mysql.md
--   PURGE_LOG
--   The PURGE_LOG procedure is used to clear materialized view logs, thereby reducing the size of the log files. Clearing materialized view logs will delete all log records related to the table.
--   Applicability
--   This topic applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition only provides MySQL-compatible mode.
--   Syntax
-- Documented syntax/signature (commented; not directly executable); fence language: sql:
--   PROCEDURE purge_log(
--       IN     master_name            VARCHAR(65535));
--   Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   | master | The name of the materialized view log table from which data needs to be purged.
--   The format is schema.object or object. |

-- [M03-0003] REFRESH
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Package/type: DBMS_MVIEW
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/300.refresh-mysql.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/300.refresh-mysql.md
--   REFRESH
--   REFRESHE is used to refresh a specified materialized view.
--   Applicability
--   This topic applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition provides only MySQL-compatible mode.
--   Syntax
-- Documented syntax/signature (commented; not directly executable); fence language: sql:
--   DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH (
--       mv_name                IN    VARCHAR(65535)
--       [, method              IN    VARCHAR(65535) DEFAULT NULL]
--       [, refresh_parallel    IN    INT DEFAULT 0]
--       [, nested              IN    BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE]
--       [, nested_refresh_mode IN    VARCHAR DEFAULT NULL]
--       [, async               IN    BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE]
--   );
--   Parameters
--   Note
--   Non-nested materialized views do not support cascading refresh. Specifying the nested and nested_refresh_mode parameters has no effect. By default, independent refresh is performed.
--   Parameter | Description
--   mv_name | The name of the materialized view to be refreshed.
--   method | The refresh strategy of the materialized view. This parameter specifies the default refresh strategy when you create a materialized view. Valid values: • F or f: fast refresh.; ?: force refresh.; C or c: complete refresh.; A or a: always refresh, which is equivalent to C.
--   refresh_parallel | The concurrency level of the refresh operation, that is, the number of threads that can be executed simultaneously.
--   nested | The refresh mode of the nested materialized view. Valid values: • FALSE: default value. Independent refresh is performed on the nested materialized view. ; TRUE: cascading refresh is performed on the nested materialized view.
--   nested_refresh_mode | The refresh behavior of cascading refresh on the nested materialized view. Valid values: • If the value of the nested parameter is FALSE, specifying the value of the nested_refresh_mode parameter will result in an error. ; If the value of the nested parameter is TRUE: • Valid values of the nested_refresh_mode parameter: • inconsistent: non-consistent cascading refresh. ; consistent: consistent cascading refresh. ; If the nested_refresh_mode parameter is not specified, non-consistent cascading refresh is performed by default.
--   async | Specifies whether to perform asynchronous refresh. Valid values: • FALSE: default value. The method returns only after the refresh task is completed. ; TRUE: The method returns immediately after creating a background refresh task upon calling REFRESH. Note This parameter is available starting with V4.4.2 BP2.
--   Examples
--   • Independent refresh:
--   • Specify only the mv_name parameter.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH('mv1');
--   • Specify the mv_name and nested parameters, and set the value of the nested parameter to false.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH(
--           'mv1',
--           nested=> false);
--   • Non-consistent cascading refresh:
--   • Specify the mv_name and nested parameters, set the value of the nested parameter to true, and do not specify the nested_refresh_mode parameter.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH(
--           'mv1',
--           nested=> true);
--   • Specify the mv_name, nested, and nested_refresh_mode parameters, set the value of the nested parameter to true, and set the value of the nested_refresh_mode parameter to inconsistent.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH(
--           'mv1',
--           nested=> true,
--           nested_refresh_mode => 'inconsistent');
--   • Consistent cascading refresh:
--   Specify the mv_name, nested, and nested_refresh_mode parameters, set the value of the nested parameter to true, and set the value of the nested_refresh_mode parameter to consistent.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH(
--         'mv1',
--         nested=> true,
--         nested_refresh_mode => 'consistent');
--   • The following examples show error scenarios:
--   • If the value of the nested parameter is false and the value of the nested_refresh_mode parameter is specified, an error will be returned.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH(
--           'mv1',
--           nested=> false,
--           nested_refresh_mode => 'xxxx');
--   • If the value of the nested parameter is not specified and the value of the nested_refresh_mode parameter is specified, an error will be returned.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH(
--           'mv1',
--           nested_refresh_mode => 'xxxx');
--   • If the value of the nested parameter is true and the value of the nested_refresh_mode parameter is an invalid string, an error will be returned.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH(
--           'mv1',
--           nested=> true,
--           nested_refresh_mode => 'illegal string');
--   References
--   For more information about refreshing materialized views, see Refresh a materialized view.

-- [M03-0004] KILL_REFRESH
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Package/type: DBMS_MVIEW
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/400.kill_refresh-mysql.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/400.kill_refresh-mysql.md
--   KILL_REFRESH
--   Note
--   For V4.4.2, the KILL_REFRESH statement is available starting from V4.4.2 BP2.
--   The KILL_REFRESH statement is used to actively terminate a materialized view refresh batch that is currently in progress and managed by the new scheduling framework.
--   Overview
--   After you call DBMS_MVIEW.KILL_REFRESH(refresh_id), the system performs the following operations on the refresh batch corresponding to the specified refresh_id:
--   • Mark tasks in the PENDING state as CANCELLED: All unscheduled tasks will be immediately canceled and will no longer enter the scheduling queue.
--   • Terminate tasks in the RUNNING state: For running tasks, the system locates their executing session and initiates a kill operation to forcibly interrupt their execution flow.
--   • Status convergence processing: Tasks that are killed are eventually marked with the status CANCELLED.
--   • Final states of tasks are not affected: Tasks that have already completed (SUCCESS), failed (FAILED), or been canceled (CANCELLED) will not be affected by this termination operation.
--   Syntax
-- Documented syntax/signature (commented; not directly executable); fence language: sql:
--   DBMS_MVIEW.KILL_REFRESH(refresh_id)
--   Parameters
--   Parameter | Note
--   refresh_id | Specifies the unique identifier for the materialized view refresh batch, which is assigned by the new scheduling framework. Note You can view the refresh_id information through the DBA_MVREF_RUN_STATS view.
--   Examples
--   Terminates the refresh of the materialized view with refresh_id 100023.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CALL DBMS_MVIEW.KILL_REFRESH(100023);
--   References
--   For more information about refreshing materialized views, see Refresh a materialized view.

-- [M03-0005] REFRESH_REPORT
-- Mode: MySQL
-- Package/type: DBMS_MVIEW
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/500.refresh_report-mysql.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/200.pl-mysql/1000.pl-system-package-mysql/9950.dbms-mview-mysql/500.refresh_report-mysql.md
--   REFRESH_REPORT
--   Note
--   For V4.4.2, REFRESH_REPORT was introduced starting from V4.4.2 BP2.
--   REFRESH_REPORT is used to generate a hierarchical diagnostic report for a single materialized view refresh task.
--   Limitations and considerations
--   Specify at least one of refresh_id and mv_name.
--   Syntax
-- Documented syntax/signature (commented; not directly executable); fence language: sql:
--   DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH_REPORT(
--       {refresh_id | mv_name}
--       [, format]
--       [, tenant_id])
--   Parameters
--   Parameter | Note
--   refresh_id | The refresh ID uniquely identifies a batch of materialized view refreshes and is assigned by the new scheduling framework. Note You can view the refresh_id information in the DBA_MVREF_RUN_STATS view.
--   mv_name | Specify the name of the materialized view (for example,SCHEMA.MV_NAME).
--   format | Optional. Specifies the report output format. Valid values: • XML(Default); JSON
--   tenant_id | Currently onlysysTenant available, used to query the materialized view refresh reports of other tenants.
--   Return value
--   • Return type: CLOB.
--   • Content format: Depends on the format parameter. It can be text in XML or JSON format.
--   Examples
--   • Retrieve the diagnostic report in XML format by using the refresh_id parameter.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   SELECT DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH_REPORT(refresh_id=> 100023);
--   • Obtain a report in JSON format.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   SELECT DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH_REPORT(refresh_id=> 100023, format => 'JSON');
--   • Retrieve the most recently refreshed report by materialized view name.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   SELECT DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH_REPORT(mv_name=> 'TEST.MV_SALES_DAILY');
--   • The sys tenant queries the refresh reports of other tenants.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   SELECT DBMS_MVIEW.REFRESH_REPORT(refresh_id=> 100023, tenant_id=> 1002);

