-- [O09-0001] Overview of DBMS_METADATA
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Package/type: DBMS_METADATA
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/300.pl-oracle/1400.pl-system-package-oracle/9900.dbms-metadata-oracle/100.dbms-metadata-overview-oracle.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/300.pl-oracle/1400.pl-system-package-oracle/9900.dbms-metadata-oracle/100.dbms-metadata-overview-oracle.md
--   Overview of DBMS_METADATA
--   The DBMS_METADATA package is used to generate DDL statements or to execute DDL statements by submitting an XML document.
--   Applicability
--   This topic applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition provides only MySQL-compatible mode.
--   Privilege requirements for DBMS_METADATA
--   The DBMS_METADATA package belongs to the SYS user and grants execute access to PUBLIC. DBMS_METADATA runs with the privileges of the caller, which are determined by the caller's security profile.
--   Overview of DBMS_METADATA subprograms
--   The following table lists the DBMS_METADATA subprograms supported in the current version of OceanBase Database and their brief descriptions.
--   Subprogram | Description |
--   GET_DDL | Returns the metadata of a single object as a DDL statement.
--   GET_TABLE_INDEX_DDL | Extracts index DDL statements for a table in batches.

-- [O09-0002] GET_DDL
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Package/type: DBMS_METADATA
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/300.pl-oracle/1400.pl-system-package-oracle/9900.dbms-metadata-oracle/200.get-ddl-oracle.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/300.pl-oracle/1400.pl-system-package-oracle/9900.dbms-metadata-oracle/200.get-ddl-oracle.md
--   GET_DDL
--   The GET_DDL function returns the metadata of a single object as a DDL statement. It is used to extract DDL (Data Definition Language) statements for database objects such as TABLE, VIEW, INDEX, PROCEDURE, SEQUENCE, TRIGGER, SYNONYM, FUNCTION, and PACKAGE.
--   The GET_xxx function is used to retrieve the metadata of an object in a single call. Currently, OceanBase Database only supports the GET_DDL function.
--   Applicability
--   This topic applies only to OceanBase Database Enterprise Edition. OceanBase Database Community Edition provides only MySQL-compatible mode.
--   Syntax
-- Documented syntax/signature (commented; not directly executable); fence language: sql:
--   FUNCTION  GET_DDL (
--       object_type     VARCHAR,
--       name            VARCHAR,
--       ob_schema       VARCHAR DEFAULT NULL,
--       version         VARCHAR DEFAULT 'COMPATIBLE',
--       model           VARCHAR DEFAULT 'ORACLE',
--       transform       VARCHAR DEFAULT 'DDL')
--   RETURN CLOB;
--   Parameters
--   Parameter | Description
--   object_type | The type of the object to retrieve. The object type must be a named object. Supported object types include TABLE, VIEW, MATERIALIZED_VIEW, INDEX, PROCEDURE, SEQUENCE, TRIGGER, SYNONYM, FUNCTION, and PACKAGE.
--   name | The name of the object.
--   ob_schema | The schema that contains the object. The default value is the schema of the caller.
--   version | The metadata version of the object.
--   model | The object model of the metadata.
--   transform | Specifies whether to apply an XSL-T transformation to the output result.
--   Examples
--   Simple examples
--   The following SQL statements demonstrate how to use GET_DDL to retrieve the DDL statements for different object types. Note that the object names in the examples, such as 'MY_TABLE_NAME', need to be replaced with actual object names in your database.
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a table (TABLE):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('TABLE', 'MY_TABLE_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a view (VIEW):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('VIEW', 'MY_VIEW_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a materialized view (MATERIALIZED VIEW):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('MATERIALIZED_VIEW', 'MY_MATERIALIZED_VIEW_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for an index (INDEX):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('INDEX', 'MY_INDEX_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a stored procedure (PROCEDURE):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('PROCEDURE', 'MY_PROCEDURE_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a sequence (SEQUENCE):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('SEQUENCE', 'MY_SEQUENCE_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a trigger (TRIGGER):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('TRIGGER', 'MY_TRIGGER_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a synonym (SYNONYM):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('SYNONYM', 'MY_SYNONYM_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a function (FUNCTION):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('FUNCTION', 'MY_FUNCTION_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   • Retrieve the DDL statement for a package (PACKAGE):
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL('PACKAGE', 'MY_PACKAGE_NAME') FROM DUAL;
--   Complete examples
--   We will show two complete examples to demonstrate how to use GET_DDL.
--   • Example 1: Create a function named tEst that accepts three parameters: an integer tEst, a string "nAMe", and a float age. The return type of the function is float.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   create function tEst(tEst int, "nAMe" varchar2, age float)
--   return float
--   as
--   begin
--     return age;
--   end;
--   /
--
--   -- Enable server output to allow messages to be printed to the console.
--   set serveroutput on;
--
--   -- Call the `DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL` function to retrieve the DDL statement for the function named 'TEST'.
--   call dbms_output.put_line(dbms_metadata.get_ddl('FUNCTION','TEST'));
--   The DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL function returns the DDL statement for creating the object.
--   The returned result is as follows:
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE "SYS"."TEST"
--   (
--    "TEST" IN int, "nAMe" IN varchar2, "AGE" IN float
--   ) IS
--   begin
--     null;
--   end
--   • Example 2: Create a stored procedure named tEst1 that accepts three parameters: an integer tEst1, a string "nAMe", and a float age.
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   create procedure tEst1(tEst1 int, "nAMe" varchar2, age float)
--   as
--   begin
--     null;
--   end;
--   /
--
--   -- Enable server output to allow messages to be printed to the console.
--   set serveroutput on;
--
--   -- Call the DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL function to retrieve the DDL statement for the stored procedure named 'TEST1'.
--   call dbms_output.put_line(dbms_metadata.get_ddl('PROCEDURE','TEST1'));
--   The returned result is the DDL statement for creating the stored procedure named TEST1.
--   The returned result is as follows:
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: shell:
--   CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE "SYS"."TEST1"
--   (
--    "TEST1" IN int, "nAMe" IN varchar2, "AGE" IN float
--   ) IS
--   begin
--     null;
--   end

-- [O09-0003] GET_TABLE_INDEX_DDL
-- Mode: Oracle
-- Package/type: DBMS_METADATA
-- Official source: https://github.com/oceanbase/oceanbase-doc/blob/017c8d35a015afa04ce1e1ce4b95675e3223c878/en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/300.pl-oracle/1400.pl-system-package-oracle/9900.dbms-metadata-oracle/300.get-table-index-ddl-oracle.md
-- Source file: en-US/700.reference/600.pl-reference/300.pl-oracle/1400.pl-system-package-oracle/9900.dbms-metadata-oracle/300.get-table-index-ddl-oracle.md
--   GET_TABLE_INDEX_DDL
--   The GET_TABLE_INDEX_DDL function is used to extract index DDL statements for a table in batches.
--   Note
--   This function is available starting from OceanBase Database V4.4.2 for V4.4.x.
--   Syntax
-- Documented syntax/signature (commented; not directly executable); fence language: sql:
--   TYPE index_ddl_row IS RECORD (
--       index_name    VARCHAR2(4000),
--       index_ddl     CLOB
--     );
--
--   TYPE index_ddl_table IS TABLE OF index_ddl_row;
--
--   FUNCTION get_table_index_ddl (
--       table_name      VARCHAR,
--       ob_schema     VARCHAR DEFAULT NULL)
--   RETURN index_ddl_table PIPELINED;
--   Parameter description
--   Parameter | Description
--   table_name | The name of the table.
--   ob_schema | The schema that contains the table. Defaults to the caller's schema.
--   Examples
--   Execute the following command to extract index DDL statements for a table in batches:
-- Official example code (commented; setup and privileges may be required); fence language: sql:
--   SELECT * FROM TABLE(DBMS_METADATA.GET_TABLE_INDEX_DDL(('TABLE_NAME', 'SCHEMA_NAME'));

